<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Muri Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1817 CE – 1903 CE (approx.) · capital Jalingo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Fulani emirate on the upper Benue in north-east Nigeria, founded as an offshoot of Gombe and long at war with the Jukun of Wukari.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Muri was founded around 1817 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamman-ruwa">Hamman Ruwa</a>, a kinsman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buba-yero">Buba Yero</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gombe-emirate">Gombe Emirate</a>, who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> forces south into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benue-river">Benue</a> valley. He established a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>, centred first on Muri, among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jukun-people">Jukun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumuye">Mumuye</a> and other peoples, and it was later recognised directly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> controlled stretches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benue-river">Benue</a> river, a growing route for trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, slaves and later palm produce. Its rulers were frequently at war with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-wukari">Kingdom of Wukari</a> and with hill peoples who resisted conquest, and succession disputes among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamman-ruwa">Hamman Ruwa's</a> descendants divided it. In the 1890s the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalingo">Jalingo</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-niger-company-territories">Royal Niger Company</a> signed treaties with Muri in the 1880s, and British forces occupied it after 1900. It became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Muri still reigns at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalingo">Jalingo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taraba-state">Taraba State</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1817 CE – 1903 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.86%;width:1.71%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1817 CE – 1903 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jalingo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hamman Ruwa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Fulfulde</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Muri</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1860&amp;focus=muri-emirate" aria-label="Muri Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4994 1845 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Muri Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5314 2101L5144 1947"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5144 1947h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5314 2101h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5314 2101h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jalingo. <a href="/atlas?year=1860&amp;focus=muri-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

